//ERR any
//BUG="nothing checks that the base of a field access is a struct"

// Not in FEEDBACK.md, and the gap left by 08_structs/59e. That case reaches an
// error, but only by accident: it prints the field access, so `print`'s
// overload set has to be resolved and the mistake surfaces as "Multiple
// candidates found for this function call". No check on the field access
// itself ever runs.
//
// `hint_is_field` (src/compiler/type_resolver/mod.rs) only records "this type
// has a child field F". It never asks whether the type is a struct, and a
// primitive that has picked up a child field is never flagged. So every shape
// that does not happen to confuse an overload set is accepted:
//
//     b.v = 5          -- writing a field of an integer
//     s: S = b.v       -- both sides fully typed
//     take(b.v)        -- passed to a function that wants an S
//     b.v              -- discarded
//
// all compile, and the compiler exits 0 having written C that gcc rejects with
// "member reference base type 'int' is not a structure or union". On a char it
// is stranger still: `c.v` comes out as `97.F0`, and gcc says "invalid suffix
// 'F0' on floating constant". The user is shown an error about generated names
// for a mistake in their own source.
//
// Found by fuzzing for programs the compiler accepts but gcc rejects, which
// also turned up 39 — a different hole in the same check.

struct S
    v: i32

fn main
    b = 2
    b.v = 5
    out "ok"
